dict(section='Introduction',
     title='Formula card · Softmax and cross-entropy loss',
     kind='text',
     bullets=['p_c = exp(z_c) ÷ Σ_j exp(z_j)     L = − log p_y', 'z_c = the logit, the raw score the head gives class c (3 classes)', 'exp = e raised to that power · Σ_j = sum over all classes j', 'p_c = predicted probability of class c; the three p_c add up to 1', 'y = the true label · L = cross-entropy loss for one tweet', 'ŷ = argmax_c p_c · uniform guess p_y = 1/3 → L = log 3 ≈ 1.10'],
     narration="Every method is trained with the same loss, so let's read it slowly. The head outputs three raw scores called logits, written z c, one for each class c. Softmax turns them into probabilities. For each class we take e to the power z c, then divide by the sum of e to the power z j over all classes j. The results are positive and add up to 1, so p c is the model's probability for class c. The loss, L, is minus the log of the probability given to the true label, y. This is called cross-entropy. If the model is sure and right, p y is 1, and the log of 1 is zero, so the loss is zero. If it guesses one third for every class, the loss is log 3, about 1.10. The prediction, y hat, is simply the class with the highest p c. Training pushes the probability of the right label up, and the loss down.",),

