dict(section='Part 1 · Tokenizers',
     title='Formula card · Fertility, UNK rate, truncation and p95',
     kind='text',
     bullets=['fertility = subword tokens ÷ whitespace words (special tokens excluded)', 'UNK rate = unknown tokens ÷ subword tokens', 'truncation rate = tweets longer than max_length ÷ all tweets', 'p95 = the length that 95% of training tweets fit inside', 'Example, XLM-R: 17,105 tokens ÷ 12,208 words = 1.401', 'max_length = the larger p95 = max(43, 41) = 43'],
     narration="Let's go through these one at a time. Fertility is the number of subword tokens divided by the number of words, where a word is anything between spaces. Special start and end tokens are not counted here. A fertility of 1 means one piece per word. Why does high fertility cost? Longer sequences mean more compute, and attention cost grows with the square of the length. More tweets get cut off, and the model sees fragments instead of whole units of meaning. For XLM-R on our training split, 17,105 tokens divided by 12,208 words gives 1.401. The unknown rate is the number of unknown tokens divided by all subword tokens. An unknown token appears when the tokenizer meets characters it cannot represent at all, so that meaning is lost. The truncation rate is the number of tweets longer than max length, divided by all tweets. And p95 is the length, counting special tokens, that 95 percent of training tweets fit inside. Our two p95 values were 43 and 41, so max length is the larger, 43.",),

