dict(section='LoRA',
     title='Method 2 · LoRA: what the handout asks',
     kind='handout',
     page=2,
     find=['LoRA, with task_type="SEQ_CLS" so that the classification head'],
     bullets=['LoRA from the PEFT library', "task_type='SEQ_CLS': sequence classification", 'The new classification head must also be trained', 'Settings come later in the table: rank 8 or 16, alpha 16'],
     narration='Method two is LoRA, short for low-rank adaptation. The handout says one specific thing: use task type SEQ underscore CLS, so that the classification head is also trained. Why does that matter? LoRA freezes the original model. But our head is brand new and random. If it were frozen too, the model could never learn to map its features to our three labels. Setting the task type to sequence classification keeps the head trainable. The rank and alpha settings come later in the experiment table: rank 8 or 16, with alpha fixed at 16, on the query and value matrices. First, the formula, then two diagrams.',),

dict(section='LoRA',
     title='Formula card · LoRA',
     kind='text',
     bullets=['h = W0 x + (α ÷ r) · B A x', 'x = input (k numbers) · h = output (d numbers)', 'W0 = frozen pre-trained weight matrix (d × k), e.g. 768 × 768', 'A = r × k (squeezes to r numbers) · B = d × r (expands back to d)', 'r = rank, 8 or 16 · α = scale, fixed at 16 → α ÷ r = 2 or 1', 'B starts at 0 → B A x = 0 at step 0: training starts from W0'],
     narration="Here is LoRA as a formula. x is the input vector, with k numbers, and h is the output, with d numbers. W zero is the frozen pre-trained weight matrix, d by k; inside attention that is 768 by 768. LoRA adds a second path. A is a small r by k matrix that squeezes the k inputs down to just r numbers. B is d by r and expands them back to d outputs. r is the rank, 8 or 16 in our experiments. Alpha is a scale, fixed at 16 by the handout, so alpha over r is 2 for rank 8 and 1 for rank 16. The two paths are added. A starts random and B starts at zero, so at the very first step B A x is zero and the model behaves exactly like the pre-trained one. Why is one of them zero and the other random? One must be zero so that B A x is zero at the start, and the other must be random so that gradients can flow. LoRA's convention is B equals zero; the reverse would also work. Alpha over r is meant to keep the update size steady when you change r; but with alpha held at 16, the scale is 2 at rank 8 and 1 at rank 16. In words: keep the big matrix, and learn a small, cheap correction to it.",),

dict(section='LoRA',
     title='Diagram · The LoRA side path',
     kind='figure',
     image='DG/lora.png',
     bullets=['Bottom path: frozen W0, 768 × 768', 'Top path: A (8 × 768, random) → B (768 × 8, zeros) → × α/r', 'The two paths are added: h = W0x + (α/r)·BAx', '12,288 per matrix · 294,912 adapters + 592,899 head = 887,811'],
     narration='Here is the LoRA formula as a picture. On the left, the input x, with k equal 768 numbers, splits into two paths. The bottom path is the big grey box, W zero, 768 by 768, frozen. The top path is LoRA. First the orange box A, 8 by 768, which starts random and squeezes the input down to only r equal 8 numbers. Then the green box B, 768 by 8, which starts at zeros and expands them back. Its output is multiplied by alpha over r, and at the circle with the plus, the two paths are added to give h. The grey box at the bottom repeats our count: 12,288 trainable numbers per matrix instead of 589,824, then 294,912 for 24 matrices, plus the head, 887,811.',),

